Research and design acknowledgments

The direct-collocation design study considered csu-hmc/opty at commit
fdad07d0f0ae149e2eedb479e7a87298fd6722ef, Copyright (c) 2014-2025,
opty authors, licensed under the BSD 2-Clause License, together with Moore and
van den Bogert, “opty: Software for trajectory optimization and parameter
identification using direct collocation,” JOSS 2(16), 300 (2017). Phydrax's
controlled-DAE contracts, normalized decision layouts, JAX sparse derivatives,
solver boundaries, certificates, tests, and examples are independent
implementations; no opty source, generated templates, fixtures, or runtime code
is copied or redistributed.

The structured nonlinear batching and iteration-pool design study considered
JAXIPM at commit 3e6d2d29cd890b7a201b037110031ec0d9487df9 and Viljoen,
Haffner, Tomizuka, and Mehr, “JAXIPM: Batched Nonlinear Programming on the
GPU.” Phydrax independently implements its structured bound-form IR, ASDEX
derivatives, KKT strategies, portable warm starts, task identity, certification,
domain audits, and execution pools; no JAXIPM source or runtime dependency is
included.

The Markov-chain-cubature design study considered tttc3/MCCube at commit
d5653d88005ef9a421f14aa0c1e9ee6cfee5cecc, licensed under the Apache License,
Version 2.0, together with the cubature-on-Wiener-space construction of Lyons
and Victoir. Phydrax's Gaussian formulas, signature certification, controlled
flow, static positive moment recombination, diagnostics, and solver contracts
are independent implementations; no MCCube source code or runtime data is
included.

The positive Gaussian formulas use the original Stroud--Secrest constructions
from *Gaussian Quadrature Formulas* (1966). The Hadamard degree-three family is
the orthogonal-array construction associated with Victoir's 2004 cubature work.

The moment-calibration formulation follows Shane Barratt, Guillermo Angeris,
and Stephen Boyd, “Optimal Representative Sample Weighting,” Statistics and
Computing 31:19 (2021), and its public cvxgrp/rsw implementation.

The absorption study also considered rswjax by Andrew Timm, licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0. The Phydrax moment-calibration implementation is
independent and does not include source code copied or translated from rsw or
rswjax.

The cubature-rule design study considered sigma-py/quadpy. Phydrax does not
include or depend on quadpy source or proprietary runtime data; its implementation
uses the original quadrature publications and the permissively licensed sources
identified above.

The bounded MAP-initialization design study considered Hyperoptax at commit
9e2950203048112bbb59aa0582b2d214cc0c16db, licensed under the Apache License,
Version 2.0. Phydrax implements a narrower sequential expected-improvement method
independently against its own kernel, Gaussian-process, posterior, search-evidence,
and state-space contracts. No Hyperoptax source code is copied or redistributed,
and randomized Kriging Believer is not implemented.

Accurate conservation accounting
The cancellation-aware conservation reductions are independent implementations of
the error-free TwoSum and Sum2 algorithms described by Takeshi Ogita, Siegfried M.
Rump, and Shin'ichi Oishi, “Accurate Sum and Dot Product,” SIAM Journal on Scientific
Computing 26(6), 1955–1988 (2005), DOI 10.1137/030601818.

The design study inspected sigma-py/accupy, licensed under GPL-3.0. Phydrax does not
copy, translate, bundle, link, import, or depend on accupy source or executables.

Compatible electromagnetics and point-cloud calculus
The compatible Maxwell, CPML, Whitney-form, adjoint, targeted-reconstruction, and
point-cloud implementations are native Phydrax implementations derived from public
mathematical specifications, including Yee's staggered Maxwell method (IEEE TAP,
1966), Roden and Gedney's convolutional PML formulation (Microwave and Optical
Technology Letters, 2000), standard Whitney differential forms, and polynomial
reproduction identities. The design study inspected OpenSBLI, Meep, juolrabi/gfd,
rmanak/FD, GFDFlow, mGFD, and fdtdx. Phydrax does not copy, translate, bundle, link,
or import their solver source, generated tables, runtime types, templates, fixtures,
or executables. GPL/CC projects remain outside the distribution; neutral adapters
accept data schemas only.
